Connections:
Front sensor

VCC  → 5 V
GND  → GND
TRIG → ESP32 GPIO 13
ECHO → ESP32 GPIO 34*

Left sensor
VCC  → 5 V
GND  → GND
TRIG → ESP32 GPIO 14
ECHO → ESP32 GPIO 35*

Right sensor
VCC  → 5 V
GND  → GND
TRIG → ESP32 GPIO 25
ECHO → ESP32 GPIO 32*

Don't directly connect a 5-V ECHO signal to GPIO 34, 35 or 32.

Use a voltage divider on each ECHO:

HC-SR04 ECHO
     │
    1 kΩ
     │
     ├──────────────→ ESP32 ECHO GPIO
     │
    2 kΩ
     │
    GND

This reduces approximately 5 V to about 3.3 V.
You need three such dividers, one for each ECHO.

Power system
Your 9-V → 5-V buck converter can supply the sensors and servo, but the power wiring should be arranged carefully.
                 9 V BATTERY
                     │
          ┌──────────┴───────────┐
          │                      │
          ▼                      ▼
     Motor driver            Buck converter
     motor supply               9 V → 5 V
          │                      │
          ▼                      ├────────→ Servo VCC
      BO motor                   ├────────→ Ultrasonic VCC
                                 └────────→ ESP32 5V/VIN*

And all grounds must be connected together:
Battery GND
     │
     ├──── Motor driver GND
     │
     ├──── Buck converter GND
     │
     ├──── ESP32 GND
     │
     ├──── Servo GND
     │
     └──── Ultrasonic GND

This common ground is essential because the ESP32's control signals need a common voltage reference.
Servo
For a conventional servo:
Buck +5V ─────────→ Servo VCC
Buck GND ─────────→ Servo GND
ESP32 GPIO ───────→ Servo SIGNAL

Do not power the servo from an ESP32 GPIO.
The servo can draw substantial current, especially when steering against resistance, so powering it from the buck converter is the correct approach.

Motor driver

#define MOTOR_PIN_1 18
#define MOTOR_PIN_2 19
#define ENA 15

ESP32                    Motor Driver
─────                    ────────────

GPIO 18 ───────────────→ IN1
GPIO 19 ───────────────→ IN2
GPIO 15 ───────────────→ ENA/PWM

GND ───────────────────→ GND

9V battery ────────────→ Motor power input

                         OUT1 ───→ BO motor
                         OUT2 ───→ BO motor

The exact motor-driver wiring depends on the particular BO motor driver module you have, so IN1/IN2/ENA should be checked against its pin labels/datasheet.

2. Why this circuit is suitable for obstacle avoidance

three ultrasonic sensors + front steering + rear-wheel drive.

The three sensors give the ESP32 spatial information about obstacles.

                 FRONT
                   ↑
              [ FRONT ]
                 SENSOR

       [LEFT]               [RIGHT]
       SENSOR                SENSOR

            ←   ROBOT   →

Instead of simply knowing "there is an obstacle", the robot can determine where the obstacle is.
For example:

Obstacle on the left
Left distance  = 15 cm
Front distance = 20 cm
Right distance = 80 cm

The ESP32 can conclude that the right side has more free space and steer toward the right.
Obstacle on the right
Left distance  = 80 cm
Front distance = 20 cm
Right distance = 15 cm

It can steer toward the left.
Obstacle directly ahead
Left distance  = 60 cm
Front distance = 15 cm
Right distance = 55 cm

The robot knows that the obstacle is primarily in its forward path and can choose a direction based on the left/right distances.
Why use three sensors instead of one?
A single front ultrasonic sensor provides essentially:
"Something is approximately 20 cm away."

Three sensors provide:
"Something is 20 cm ahead,
there is 15 cm of clearance on the left,
and 70 cm of clearance on the right."

That information makes the steering decision much better.
Why front-wheel steering is advantageous
Your robot is essentially using a steering-wheel configuration:
             FRONT
               ↑
          ┌─────────┐
          │ Steering│
          │  wheel  │
          └────┬────┘
               │
             BODY
               │
        ┌──────┴──────┐
        │ Rear wheels │
        │ + BO motor  │
        └─────────────┘

The servo controls the direction of the front wheel while the BO motor provides propulsion.
This separates the two functions:

BO motor → forward/reverse movement
Servo → steering direction
That is relatively simple to control and closely resembles the steering mechanism of a conventional vehicle.
Why put the ESP32 in charge of the sensors and actuators?
This is where Raspberry Pi master + ESP32 slave architecture becomes useful.
The Raspberry Pi can perform computationally intensive tasks:

Camera
   ↓
OpenCV
   ↓
Image processing
   ↓
Object/color recognition
   ↓
Decision
   ↓
"dodgeright()" / "dodgeleft()"

The ESP32 handles the physical robot:
Ultrasonic sensors
       ↓
Distance information
       ↓
Obstacle decision
       ↓
Servo + motor

So the Pi doesn't have to directly generate precise motor/servo control signals.
The resulting architecture
             RASPBERRY PI
          ┌───────────────┐
          │ Camera        │
          │ OpenCV        │
          │ Vision        │
          │ High-level    │
          │ decisions     │
          └───────┬───────┘
                  │
             UART/Serial
                  │
                  ▼
              ESP32
       ┌───────────────────┐
       │                   │
       │  Ultrasonic       │
       │  sensors          │
       │       ↓           │
       │  Obstacle logic   │
       │       ↓           │
       │  Steering servo   │
       │       +           │
       │  Motor driver     │
       │                   │
       └───────────────────┘

This is a strong design because the Pi makes higher-level decisions while the ESP32 handles real-time physical control.
